<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 18, 2023">
<CPscript title="Experimenting with Extremal Length" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>The extremal length between two arcs on the boundary of a region can be extimated by mapping the region to a rectangle with the images of the arcs as its ends. The discrete analogue is illustrated here.</description>
<text> "Extremal Length" is a classical conformal invariant, a quantity attached to a region which remains unchanged under conformal mappings. The notion we consider is described in Section 6.4.4 of the book. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="idea.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read cookied.p;infile_path region.g;Disp -w -c -g;copy 1; </cmd>
<text> Our formulation involves a simply connected region G in the plane and two designated boundary arcs. Though the formal definition is too intricate to describe here, the invariance gives us a way to define the discrete analogue, and in fact, to approximate the numerical value. </text>
<Section title="The Construction">
<text> We start with the discrete version of our region, namely, a circle packing approximating G. The next command identifies and color-codes two boundary arcs. [The user is encouraged to modify the 'set_Vlist' command by selecting a new set of 4 counterclockwise oriented boundary vertices.] </text>
<cmd iconname="idea.png">act 0;set_Vlist 770 686 715 733;[e]; </cmd>
<text> The conformal map we exploit is one that carries G to a rectangle, with the two arcs going to its ends. This may take a moment to compute, it will appear in p1. The extremal length of the rectangle is simply width/height, and conformal invariance tells us that this is the extremal length of the arcs with respect to G as well. 

If you want to know the computed numerical value, open the "Advanced" frame, press the "Messages" button: there will be a message (in blue) giving "log(width/height)". The closer the rectangle is to a square, the closer this value is to 0. </text>
<cmd iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 1;set_aim 1.0 b;set_aim .5 Vlist;repack;layout;norm_scale -h Vlist[1] Vlist[2];set_screen -a;disp -w -c -e b;[e];aspect Vlist; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Challenges">
<text> We pose three questions regarding extremal length. Careful thought should allow you to guess the answers. Only then should you arrange the actual experiments and see if you are right.

QUESTION 1: Suppose the blue arc is extended as the next command shows. Will that increase or decrease the extremal length between the blue and red arcs? (Explain your answer.) The optional commad 'a' will do the computation in p2 and let you compare it to the original in p1. </text>
<cmd iconname="bookmark.png">act 0;set_Vlist 770 700 715 733;[e];cleanse -p2;Map 0 2 </cmd>
<text> QUESTION 2: Suppose we revert to the original blue and red arcs, but now we modify the region G, as the next command shows. What is the effect on extremal length? (Explain your answer.) The optional command 'a' will do the computation in p2 and let you compare it to the original in p1. </text>
<cmd iconname="centrejust.png">act 0;cleanse -p0;infile_read bulged.p;infile_path plus.g;Disp -w -c -g;set_Vlist 812 726 755 773;[e];cleanse -p2;Map 0 2; </cmd>
<text> QUESTION 3: The complementary arcs in the boundary of G are shown in the next command as green and yellow. What is the extremal length in G between green and yellow? (Explain your reasoning.) The last command does the computation in p2. Look in the "Message" window for the results here and earlier and explain their relationship. Visually comparing p2 to p1 may help. </text>
<cmd iconname="psi.png">act 0;cleanse -p0;cleanse -p2;Map 0 2;infile_read cookied.p;infile_path region.g;Disp -w -c -g;set_Vlist 770 686 715 733;[e];[c]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kivio.png">act 2;copy -p0 2;set_aim 1.0 b;set_aim .5 Vlist;repack;layout;norm_scale -h Vlist[1] Vlist[2];set_screen -a;disp -w -c -e b;[e];[c];aspect 686 715 733 770;Map 1 2; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="e" mnemonic="e" iconname="bookmark.png">set_elist b(Vlist[0] Vlist[1]);disp -ec5t10 elist;set_elist b(Vlist[2] Vlist[3]);disp -ec195t10 elist; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="a" mnemonic="a" tooltip="do the computation in p2" iconname="format_increaseindent.png">act 2;copy -p0 2;set_aim 1.0 b;set_aim .5 Vlist;repack;layout;norm_scale -h Vlist[1] Vlist[2];set_screen -a;disp -w -c -e b;[e];aspect Vlist;Map 1 2 </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" iconname="xeyes.png">set_elist b(Vlist[1] Vlist[2]);disp -ec233t10 elist;set_elist b(Vlist[3] Vlist[0]);disp -ec209t10 elist; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
